  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It passes the typecheck, but I haven't run `vite build` or looked at a rendered frame, because there is no shell or browser here.\n\n- **Sphere:** a `material.custom` GLSL shader on a sphere with diameter 2.1. It has a cyan fresnel rim, horizontal world-space scanlines that scroll upward with a `uClock` uniform, and a translucent body (alpha 0.07 up to about 0.95 at the rim).\n- **Cube:** a metallic cube of size 0.62 sits inside the sphere and rotates each frame. It has a neutral environment so the metal reflects something.\n- **Background and floor:** the background is near-black. The floor is a dark, glossy plane with a cyan point light at the sphere's center. That light is what puts the glow on the floor. There is also a light bloom effect.\n- **Camera:** a perspective camera frames the sphere.\n- **Rendering contract:** it draws into `#app` and renders continuously. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one animation frame following it, so the first frame has been drawn by then.\n\nI couldn't confirm the shader compiles. A GLSL compile error would only show up in the browser console as `CUSTOM_SHADER_COMPILE_FAILED`, and that draw would be skipped silently.\n\n",
